---
/**
 * Privacy.
 *
 * Written to describe what this site actually does, rather than pasted from a
 * boilerplate policy. It is static pages plus one first-party script that
 * counts visits (src/lib/tracker.ts, collected by functions/api/e.ts into the
 * table in migrations/). No cookies, no third-party scripts, no forms.
 *
 * THIS PAGE FOLLOWS THE FEATURE. tests/invariants/privacy-disclosure.test.ts
 * holds a disclosure here for every column the visit log stores, the
 * retention period the collector enforces (RETENTION_MONTHS, derived from
 * RETENTION_DAYS, never typed), the daily visitor code, and the opt-out the
 * shipped beacon honours. Adding a column without describing it here fails
 * that test.
 *
 * Written for the chapter's readers, many of them older veterans: short
 * sentences, everyday words, no "telemetry" or "pseudonymous".
 */
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import ReviewNote from '../components/ReviewNote.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
import { RETENTION_MONTHS } from '../data/analytics';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this website does and does not collect."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  lede="Short version: this website counts visits to its pages. It sets no cookies, stores nothing on your device, and does not keep your IP address."
  width="prose"
>
  <h2>What we record when you visit</h2>
  <p>
    When a page of this website opens in your browser, a small piece of code that belongs to this website, not to
    an advertising or analytics company, sends a short note back to the website. The chapter keeps one record for
    each page you view, and that record holds only these things:
  </p>
  <ul class="disclosures">
    <li><strong>Which page you looked at on this website</strong>, for example the Get Help page.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The website that sent you here</strong>, if your browser says, such as a search engine or Facebook. If you
      came from another page of this website, the record shows which page that was. Either way, anything after a
      question mark in the address is removed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your approximate location: city, state and country</strong>, as Cloudflare, the company that delivers
      this website, works it out from your internet connection. It is often only roughly right, and it is never a
      street address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The name of your internet provider</strong>, such as Comcast or Verizon, and the number that identifies
      its network, also as Cloudflare works it out.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The size of your browser window</strong>, which tells us whether people read on phones or computers.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>How long the page was open and visible on your screen.</strong> Time it spent hidden behind another tab
      or app is not counted.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The date and time of the visit.</strong></li>
    <li>
      <strong>A visitor code that changes every day</strong>, explained below, and <strong>a random number for that one
      page view</strong>, which lets the record be updated while you read.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Everything else on this website comes from this website too: the fonts, the pictures and the counting code. No
    other company&rsquo;s scripts, ads or tracking pixels are loaded, and there are no forms that send information
    to us.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not keep</h2>
  <ul class="disclosures">
    <li>
      <strong>Your IP address</strong>, the number that identifies your internet connection. Cloudflare reads it to work
      out the approximate location and provider above, and it is used to make the daily visitor code, but it is never
      stored.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cookies.</strong> This website sets none for visitors. (The private page for the chapter&rsquo;s
      administrators uses a sign-in cookie, for them only.)
    </li>
    <li><strong>Anything on your device.</strong> Nothing is saved in your browser, so there is nothing to clear.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your activity on other websites.</strong> This website does not follow you anywhere else, and it shares
      nothing with advertisers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The daily visitor code</h2>
  <p>
    So that three pages read by one person count as one visitor rather than three, each record carries a code made by
    scrambling your IP address and your browser&rsquo;s name together with the date and a secret key that only this
    website&rsquo;s server holds. Without that key, the code cannot be traced back to your internet connection.
    Because the date is part of it, the code changes every day, so the code by itself cannot link a visit on one day
    to a visit on another.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other details in a record, such as your town, your internet provider and the size of your browser window, are
    kept as they are, so someone who visits often from a small place may still be recognizable from them.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can see it, and for how long</h2>
  <p>
    Only the chapter&rsquo;s website administrators, on a private page that asks them to sign in first. The records are
    stored for the chapter by Cloudflare. The chapter does not sell them or share them with anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Records are kept for {RETENTION_MONTHS} months. Once a record is more than {RETENTION_MONTHS} months old, it is
    deleted automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>How to turn it off</h2>
  <p>
    Many browsers have a privacy setting called <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong>, or an older one called
    <strong>Do Not Track</strong>. If either is turned on in your browser, the counting code on this website does
    nothing at all: no note is sent, and no record of your visit is made. Turning off JavaScript in your browser also
    stops it, and the rest of this website works the same without it.
  </p>

  <h2>What our host can see</h2>
  <p>
    The site is served by Cloudflare, which keeps standard web-server records — IP address, timestamp, the page
    requested, browser type — for security and reliability. We do not have access to those records as individual
    profiles and we do not use them to identify anyone.
  </p>

  <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
  <p>
    Pages here link to the Department of Veterans Affairs, the Veterans Crisis Line, Facebook, payment services and
    other veterans organizations. Once you follow one of those links you are on their site, under their privacy
    policy, not ours.
  </p>

  <h2>If you contact us</h2>
  <p>
    If you call, write or email the chapter, that correspondence is handled by chapter volunteers. It is used to
    answer you, and to administer membership or assistance where that is what you asked about. The chapter does not
    sell or rent contact information.
  </p>

  <h2>Members and privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The chapter lists its officers and directors, and the people who look after a particular job for it, such as
    membership or meeting reservations, because those are public roles. Each is listed with the email address they use
    for the chapter, and some of those are personal addresses. The site does not publish other members&rsquo; contact
    details, home addresses or donation amounts.
  </p>

  <ReviewNote topic="This policy needs the chapter's approval">
    <p>
      This text describes what the rebuilt site does technically. It has not been reviewed by the chapter and is not
      legal advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chapter also has an existing privacy policy on the current site which should be compared against this one,
      and a decision is needed about whether to publish members&rsquo; names, donors&rsquo; names, and personal
      contact details at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      The visit counting described above is new. It was added after the chapter last reviewed this site, and it needs
      the chapter&rsquo;s sign-off, both for the counting itself and for this description of it.
    </p>
  </ReviewNote>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Write to VVA Chapter 227, {site.mailingAddress.line1}, {site.mailingAddress.city}, {site.mailingAddress.state}{' '}
    {site.mailingAddress.zip}, or call <a href={`tel:${site.phone.replace(/-/g, '')}`}>{site.phone}</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6);
    margin-block-end: var(--s-3);
  }

  h2:first-of-type {
    margin-block-start: 0;
  }

  /* The global reset zeroes every margin, so a section with more than one
     block (a paragraph, then the list of what is recorded, then another
     paragraph) needs its spacing stated. */
  p + p,
  p + .disclosures,
  .disclosures + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-3);
  }

  .disclosures {
    padding-inline-start: var(--s-5);
  }

  .disclosures li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-2);
  }
</style>
